A.OUT(5)                       P32W Unix                        A.OUT(5)

NAME
       a.out - executable and object file formats

DESCRIPTION
       ld(1) writes executables in the a.out format below, and
       as(1) and cc(1) -c write relocatable objects (.o files) in
       the P32O format below; ar(1) collects objects into archives
       (ar(5)). The host tools (assembler/p32asm.py, p32ld.py,
       p32ar.py) write the same bytes as the native ones. All words
       are 32 bits, little-endian.

       The executable. An a.out starts with a header of eight
       words:

              0   magic        0x50333277 (bytes "w23P")
              4   text size    bytes
              8   data size    bytes
              12  bss size     bytes
              16  entry        the address execution starts at
              20  flags        0
              24  symbols      size of the symbol table in bytes
              28  0

       Then follow text size bytes of text, data size bytes of
       data (straight after the text, not padded to a page), and
       the symbol table.

       Text is linked at 0x1000 (page 0 is never mapped, so a null
       pointer faults); data starts at the next 4 KiB page boundary
       after the text, and bss follows the data, 4-aligned. The
       linker defines _text, _etext, _data, _edata, _bss and _end
       (the first free byte after the bss) and _data_load; the
       entry is _start (in /usr/lib/crt0.o) unless ld -e names
       another symbol.

       Loading. exec(2) fails with ENOEXEC unless the magic is
       right and the entry lies inside the text. It maps the text
       read-only and executable at 0x1000 and the data and bss
       read-write at the next page, copies text and data in from
       the file, and leaves the bss zero. The break (the end of
       the heap) starts after the bss. The stack is at the top of
       the 8 MiB address space, below 0x00800000, and may grow to
       1 MiB.
       At entry the stack pointer points at argc, followed by the
       argv pointers, a null pointer, the envp pointers and a null
       pointer; the strings lie above them. At most 64 arguments
       and 64 environment strings, 4096 bytes of strings in all,
       are passed (E2BIG). A set-user-id or set-group-id file runs
       with its owner's or group's id as the effective id. The
       symbol table is not loaded.

       The symbol table. Unless ld -s was given, every symbol of
       every object linked, local and global, and the linker's
       own, sorted by value, then name, then type. Each entry is

              value (word), type (byte), name length (byte), name

       padded with zeros to a multiple of 4 bytes. The type is T
       (text), D (data), B (bss) or A (absolute), in lower case
       for a local symbol. db(1) reads it.

       The object. A relocatable object starts with twelve words:

              0   "P32O"       4  version (2)
              8   text size    12 data size    16 bss size
              20  text align   24 data align   28 bss align
              32  nsyms        36 nrelocs
              40  string table size
              44  the source file name (a string table offset)

       then, each part padded with zeros to a multiple of 4: the
       text bytes; the data bytes; nsyms symbols of 12 bytes

              name (string table offset), value (word),
              section (byte), global (byte), 0 (2 bytes)

       where section is 0 absolute, 1 .text, 2 .data, 3 .bss and
       value is the offset in the section; nrelocs relocations of
       16 bytes

              section (byte), kind (byte), 0 (2 bytes),
              offset (word), symbol (string table offset),
              addend (signed word)

       and the string table of NUL-terminated names. Symbols are
       sorted by name (byte order). The string table holds the
       source name, the symbol names in order, then each
       relocation's symbol on its first use.

       Relocations. A relocation patches the word at offset in its
       section (.text or .data). Its symbol is a section name
       (.text, .data, .bss: that section of this object) or a
       global symbol of any object. With S the symbol's final
       address, A the addend and P the address of the word:

              0  ABS32   word = S + A
              1  HI16    low 16 bits = (S + A) >> 16       (lui)
              2  LO16    low 16 bits = (S + A) & 0xFFFF    (ori)
              3  J24     low 24 bits = (S + A - (P + 4)) / 4
                                                    (jmp, call)

       LO16 is used with ori, which zero-extends, so no carry
       adjustment is needed. A J24 target must be word aligned
       and within 32 MiB of the jump.

FILES
       a.out          ld's default output
       /usr/lib/crt0.o
                      the start-up code: _start
       /usr/lib/libc.a
                      the C library

SEE ALSO
       ld(1), as(1), cc(1), ar(1), ar(5), db(1), exec(2)

BUGS
       There is no shared text: each process has its own copy of
       its program. Debugging information is limited to the
       symbol table (no line numbers or types). Symbol names
       longer than 255 bytes are cut short in the executable's
       table.
